Identify all ports that is running on the target system

nmap -Pn <target>
When we want to scan the Windows system or any systems that is blocking 
our traditionals host discoveries prove, we need to use the "Pn" option, 
that means don't use ping scan, What this does, is to skip the Host Discovery part
and perform a port scan.

nmap -F <target> //NMAP fast scan option
nmap -Pn -p- //Indicate -p- to scan all ports (scanning range 1~65535)

Typically working theory of TCP SYN Scan (-sS)
PORT STATUS: OPEN
1. NMAP -> SYN -> Target
2. Target -> SYN+ACK -> NMAP
PORT STATUS: CLOSED
1. NMAP -> SYN -> Target
2. Target -> RST -> NMAP
PORT STATUS: FILTERED
1. NMAP -> SYN -> Target
2. No response...

The primary reason that consider the -sS scanning is stealthier scanning option, is because when we receive the
syn+ack packet from the port that is runnning on the target system, nmap will send a RST packet to finish the connection, 
and cause it doen't complete the 3-way-handshake process. Its result this scanning option avoid the creation of connection log
entries on the target system. Most O.S. log the creation of TCP session (Full TCP 3-way-handshake).
